  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA large-scale low pressure system is currently tracking through\nthe Great Lakes early this morning. Over the last 24 hours two \nmesoscale convective systems (MCS) have developed, one over Iowa\nand currently tracking into Missouri and one north of Lake \nErie. Recycling of moisture from these remnant MCS's will likely \nredevelop showers and thunderstorms in the next 4 to 12 hours \nacross the Ohio River Valley. Warm and humid air (low 70s dew \npoints) fuel instability estimates that range from 1500 J/kg on \nthe low end to reasonably high end expectations near 2500 J/kg. \nThis in combination with strong wind shear (35-45 kts of 0-6km \nshear) will create a favorable environment for organized \nthunderstorms. The Enhanced Risk that has been issued by the\nStorm Prediction Center 2 days ago is still in effect with\nlittle to no change. \n\nThe most likely timing of severe weather remains between \n12-8pm. Models suggest activity begins late morning or midday \nalong a remnant outflow boundary. Storms are then increasingly \nfavored further south and east across Pittsburgh and the I-70 \ncorridor into the afternoon before convective coverage begins to\nwane after sunset. \n\nThe primary threat from storms today remains damaging wind \ngusts along some kind of developing squall line although \ntornadoes and large hail remain possible in isolated supercells \nthat could develop out in front of the main line. \n\nAnother threat from this activity remains heavy rain. PWATs are\nexpected to be above the 95th percentile and could reach up \ntowards the daily maximum values (1.96 inches). This accompanied\nwith a relatively high freezing level will allow for efficient \nwarm rain processes in a very moist environment. Therefore, rainfall\nrates could range between 3-6 inches per hour at times. Storms \nare expected to be rather progressive, which would favor flash \nflood concerns requiring training or multiple rounds of rain in \nthe same area. A Flood Watch has been issued for portions of \nour region between 1pm and 11pm today due to this noted threat.\nCounties could be added or removed through the evolution of this\nevent. \n\nExpect lingering showers and non-severe thunderstorms into \nearly Wednesday before a cold front tracks through the region.\n\nThe potential for precipitation remain lows through the \nremainder of the work week and into the weekend before showers \nand storms again look possible on Sunday.",
